---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/weathering-erosion-and-landforms">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson builds on two you may already have met: plate tectonics (which lifts
    mountains) and the water cycle (which supplies the rivers that tear them down). You
    need no other background — only the willingness to look at a landscape and refuse to
    see it as permanent.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the difference between weathering, erosion and deposition, and give an example of each;</li>
    <li>list the main mechanical and chemical weathering processes, and say why limestone landscapes dissolve while granite ones crumble;</li>
    <li>match each transport agent — water, wind, ice, waves, gravity — to the landform signature it leaves;</li>
    <li>sort the three families of sedimentary rock, and read a cliff of layers as a sentence of history;</li>
    <li>interpret sedimentary structures — cross-bedding, ripples, mud cracks, fossils — as records of ancient environments.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever a landscape appears — a valley, a cliff, a beach — ask <em>which
    process made this shape, and is it still working now?</em> The shape names the process.
  </p>

  <h2>A demolition project 4.5 billion years old</h2>
  <p>
    The surface of the Earth is a demolition and redistribution project that has been
    running for 4.5 billion years. Tectonics builds mountains; three processes tear them
    down and file the pieces. <strong>Weathering</strong> breaks rock apart <em>in
    place</em> — with ice, water chemistry, temperature swings and roots.
    <strong>Erosion</strong> picks the pieces up and moves them — by rivers, glaciers,
    wind and waves. <strong>Deposition</strong> sets them down again — in deltas, dune
    fields and sea floors — sorted by size, layer by layer. Every landscape is a report
    on which process dominated, and for how long.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scale deserves a pause. The Grand Canyon is a kilometre of rock removed by one
    river and its tributaries. The Himalaya's erosion feeds the Bengal Fan, a cone of
    sediment 16 km thick on the Indian Ocean floor — the largest body of sediment on the
    planet. Nothing humans build compares; yet the same processes, sped up, are what
    farmers and erosion-control engineers fight every season. Geomorphology — the study
    of landforms — is the grammar for reading all of it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="call the size of the planet's biggest sediment pile" widget="estimate" config={weatherFanEst} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Estimate before revealing: the Bengal Fan, fed by the Himalaya's erosion, is about 16 km
    thick — the largest body of sediment on the planet, built entirely from one mountain
    range's demolition. Notice the scale reference: the Grand Canyon is a kilometre of rock
    removed, and this undersea cone holds sixteen times that depth.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="break, move, sort, read" widget="equationStep" config={weatherCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four panels are the lesson's spine, in order.
    Panel 1 takes rock apart in place; panel 2 moves the pieces; panel 3 sorts what
    settles; panel 4 hands you the decoder ring — every classic landform is the signature
    of one process. When you reach the sections below, keep matching each one back to its
    panel: the lesson is one demolition crew, watched four ways.
  </p>

  <h2>Mechanical weathering: rock taken apart, chemistry unchanged</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Mechanical (physical) weathering</strong> breaks rock without changing what it
    is made of. The champion is <strong>frost wedging</strong>: water seeps into cracks
    and expands about 9% when it freezes, prying with forces up to roughly 200 MPa —
    enough to split granite. Repeat nightly through a mountain winter and cliff faces
    shed loose angular rubble, called scree. Its cousins: <strong>thermal
    cycling</strong> (daily heating and cooling stresses minerals that expand at
    different rates — the desert's shatter), <strong>exfoliation</strong> (when erosion
    removes the weight above, buried granite expands outward and peels in onion sheets —
    the domes of Half Dome and Sugarloaf), <strong>abrasion</strong> (grains
    sandblasting each other), and <strong>root wedging</strong> (a growing root widening
    a crack with slow, patient force).
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="file each process under the weathering it belongs to" widget="sort-buckets" config={weatherProcSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> File each process by its test — did the chemistry change? Frost wedging, exfoliation domes
    and root wedging stay mechanical; feldspar rotting into clay, calcite dissolving into
    karst and oxidation rusting iron minerals consume minerals outright. Notice that the
    buckets feed each other: mechanical weathering multiplies the surfaces chemical
    weathering then attacks.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drop the marker — the frost wedge's leverage" widget="place" config={weatherFrostPlace} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Drop the marker near the surprising number: water expands about 9% when it freezes, prying
    with forces up to roughly 200 MPa — enough to split granite. Notice the schedule that
    makes it lethal: repeated nightly through a mountain winter, which is how patient
    physics sheds a cliff face as angular scree.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notice what every one of these needs: a <em>crack</em> to work in. Weathering is a
    business of surfaces — the more broken the rock, the faster it breaks. That fact
    becomes the engine of the next section.
  </p>

  <h2>Chemical weathering: rock taken apart, chemistry changed</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Chemical weathering</strong> attacks the minerals themselves, and rainwater is
    the reagent. Rain is not pure water: as it falls it dissolves carbon dioxide, forming
    weak carbonic acid. Three reactions do most of the work.
    <strong>Dissolution</strong> simply dissolves soluble minerals: halite and gypsum go
    quickly; <strong>calcite</strong>, the mineral of limestone, goes slowly but
    inexorably — which is why limestone terrains develop <strong>karst</strong>, a
    landscape of caves, sinkholes and disappearing streams, and why marble statues
    weather faster than granite ones. <strong>Hydrolysis</strong> attacks silicates:
    feldspar, the most abundant mineral family in the crust, rots into clay minerals plus
    dissolved ions carried away by rivers — this single reaction is why clay is the most
    abundant weathering product on Earth. <strong>Oxidation</strong> rusts iron-bearing
    minerals, supplying the reds and browns of weathered rock and soil.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each reaction with its landscape signature" widget="match" config={weatherChemMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each reaction with its signature and start at the reagent: rain absorbs CO₂ as it
    falls, becoming weak carbonic acid. Then dissolution writes karst — caves, sinkholes and
    disappearing streams — hydrolysis rots feldspar into clay, and oxidation supplies the
    reds and browns of weathered rock. Watch each left card name the reagent and each right
    card the evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two kinds of weathering feed each other. Frost cracks give acid water fresh
    surfaces to attack; chemical rot crumbles rock so ice can split it further — a
    feedback that demolishes whole mountainsides, and the reason fresh granite
    <em>corestones</em> can sit inside rotten-granite <em>saprolite</em> on tropical
    hills. And the products are sorted by their resistance: quartz — which makes up under
    20% of the crust — shrugs off weak acids and oxygen, is hard, and has no cleavage, so
    it survives as sand grains; the feldspars become clay and dissolved ions; everything
    else joins the dissolved load. Quartz sand, clay, and ions in solution are the three
    great products of weathering — the first two pile up on continents, the ions and some
    clays wash out to the sea floor.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "rain is pure water, and pure water barely weathers
  rock." Rain is already dilute carbonic acid — it dissolves atmospheric CO₂ as it
  falls — so even clean rain is a chemical reagent, and pollution can drop its pH
  further still. The karst landscapes exist because ordinary rain, over millennia, is an
  acid bath; nothing exotic is required.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "weathering and erosion are the same thing." They are
  separate stages: weathering is <em>in place</em> (the rock is weakened and fragmented
  where it sits); erosion is <em>transport</em> (the pieces are carried away). A
  crumbled but unmoved talus slope shows weathering without erosion; a polished,
  clean-cut glacial valley shows erosion that outran weathering. Both words describe the
  demolition, but they name different jobs on the crew.</p>

  <h2>Erosion: the transport fleet, agent by agent</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Water</strong> is the planet's chief hauler. A stream erodes by hydraulic
    action (water hammering into cracks), abrasion (its sediment load sandpapering the
    bed) and solution, and it carries the results three ways: <em>bedload</em> rolling
    along the bottom, <em>suspended load</em> drifting with the current, and
    <em>dissolved load</em> invisible in the water itself.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blanks — the three ways a stream hauls" widget="cloze" config={weatherLoadCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Fill the blanks from the hint — one rolls, one rides, one vanishes: bedload rolls along the
    bottom, the suspended load drifts with the current, and the dissolved load is invisible
    in the water itself. Notice that the three loads carry weathering's three great products
    — quartz sand, clay and ions in solution — downstream in plain sight.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ice</strong> is the bulldozer. Glacier ice, under hundreds of metres of its
    own weight, drags frozen debris across bedrock, <em>plucking</em> blocks loose and
    <em>polishing</em> what remains with rock-flour. Its signature is unmistakable:
    <strong>U-shaped valleys</strong> (versus the stream's V), cirques, arêtes, fjords —
    and deposits with no sorting at all. Moraine and till are a chaotic mix from clay to
    house-sized boulders, the one sediment where the size law conspicuously fails,
    because ice does not sort.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the backwards claim — what ice refuses to do" widget="spot-error" config={weatherIceSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Find the backwards claim: moraine and till are a chaotic mix from clay to house-sized
    boulders — ice does not sort. Notice the flip side: the size law belongs to water, so a
    well-sorted gravel-to-clay sequence is the signature of a current, while unsorted chaos
    is the glacier's fingerprint.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Wind</strong> is the specialist of dry, unvegetated places. It can win only
    sand and dust, but it works them hard: it builds <strong>dunes</strong> whose
    inclined layers record the prevailing wind, and it has blown <strong>loess</strong> —
    vast silt blankets derived from glacial outwash — into deposits hundreds of metres
    thick on China's loess plateau, which became, ironically, superb farmland.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Waves</strong> run the coastal department. When waves approach an irregular
    shore, the parts that feel shallow water slow down first, so the wave crests bend —
    <em>refraction</em> — and the energy is focused onto the protruding
    <strong>headlands</strong> rather than the bays between them. Headlands therefore
    erode faster, and an irregular coast slowly straightens itself, over millions of
    years, into sea cliffs, <strong>wave-cut platforms</strong> (flat surfaces cut across
    the surf zone) and the surviving towers of resistant rock called
    <strong>stacks</strong>. The sequence is watchable in one cliff: a sea cave eroded
    from both sides becomes an <strong>arch</strong>; when the arch collapses, its
    pillar is left standing as a stack.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="rebuild the coast's slow straightening" widget="order" config={weatherCoastOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Order the coast's slow straightening: waves refract and focus their energy on the protruding
    headland, a sea cave erodes from both sides, the cave cuts through to an arch, and the
    arch collapses to leave a resistant stack. Notice the driver throughout — refraction
    aims the attack at headlands, which is how an irregular shore flattens into cliffs and
    platforms over millions of years.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "deserts, having no water, barely weather." They
  simply switch agents: thermal cycling stresses rock daily, rare cloudbursts do decades
  of water work in an hour, wind abrades and sorts, and salt crystallising in pores
  pricks grains apart — many desert pavements and honeycomb <em>tafoni</em> are
  weathering products, not absences of it. Aridity slows the chemistry; it never stops
  the demolition.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> So far: the transport fleet divides the work — water hauls in three loads, ice bulldozes
    without sorting, wind works sand and dust, waves focus on headlands — and every agent
    leaves a signature landform that names it.
  </p>

  <h2>Deposition: the size law and where sediment comes to rest</h2>
  <p>
    Every transport agent has a breaking point, and when it passes one, it drops its
    cargo — biggest first. Fast flows carry big particles; slow flows only fine ones; so
    wherever a current brakes, it <strong>deposits by size</strong>, and downstream
    sorting runs gravel → sand → silt → clay, with the dissolved load reaching the sea
    last. That one law builds every <strong>alluvial fan</strong> (a mountain stream
    braking onto flat ground), every <strong>floodplain</strong> (a river overflowing and
    dropping its silt), every <strong>delta</strong> (a river meeting still water and
    filing its entire load), and — applied by the sea — every beach, wave-sorted sand
    between mudflat and cliff.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="run the flood — decide what settles where" widget="branch" config={weatherFloodBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Branch the flood and apply the size law: coarse gravel drops right at the channel where the
    flow brakes, clay stays suspended longest and settles farthest out, and the floodplain
    keeps its silt either way — which is why floodplains are farmland. Notice the jar test
    in the feedback: big grains first, clay last, wherever a current slows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Deposited sediment does not stay loose. Burial compacts it, and minerals
    precipitating between the grains — usually calcite, silica or iron oxide — cement it
    into rock. The whole transformation is called <strong>lithification</strong>, and it
    is the doorway into the next section: once sediment is rock, it becomes a document.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> weathering demolishes rock in place, the transport fleet
    moves the debris, and the size law sorts what settles. The result — layer upon layer
    of sorted, cemented sediment — is the raw material of sedimentary rocks, and those
    rocks can be read.
  </p>

  <h2>Sedimentary rock types: three clans</h2>
  <p>
    Lithified sediment sorts into three clans. <strong>Clastic</strong> rocks are made of
    broken rock particles, classified by grain size: <strong>conglomerate</strong>
    (rounded gravel — a fast, short journey), <strong>sandstone</strong> (sand —
    deserts, beaches, rivers, and overwhelmingly quartz, the survivor mineral),
    <strong>shale</strong> (mud compacted into fissile sheets — the calm deep-water
    record, and the most abundant sedimentary rock of all). <strong>Chemical</strong>
    rocks precipitated from solution: <strong>limestone</strong> (calcite from warm
    shallow seas — much of it shell debris), <strong>evaporites</strong> (halite and
    gypsum left behind by drying basins — the Mediterranean nearly dried to one about 6
    million years ago, leaving kilometre-thick salt), and chert (silica).
    <strong>Organic</strong> rocks are biology-made: <strong>coal</strong> (compacted
    swamp forest, carbonised), <strong>chalk</strong> (the coccolithophore chalk skies
    of the Cretaceous), and reef limestones. Every sedimentary rock, in other words, is
    a compressed photograph of a place and moment, stacked in order.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each rock with its origin" widget="match" config={weatherRockMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each rock with its origin and read the journey in the grain: conglomerate's rounded
    gravel records a fast, short trip, shale's fissile sheets the calm deep-water record —
    and the most abundant sedimentary rock of all. Notice the two odd clans: limestone is
    calcite and shell debris from warm shallow seas, coal is compacted swamp forest.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read a cliff as a worked example. Bottom to top: a coarse conglomerate with rounded
    pebbles, fining upward into cross-bedded sandstone, then shale carrying mud cracks
    and ripple marks, capped by fossiliferous limestone. The reading: a fast mountain
    stream dropping its gravel, migrating to a braided river and dune field (the
    sandstone), then a quiet floodplain lake drying at its margins (the shale), finally
    a warm shallow sea moving in (the limestone). Five rock names, one sentence of
    history — the land sank or the sea rose, in that order — with superposition
    (younger layers on top) supplying the timeline the whole way up.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="bet before you read on — the cliff's one-sentence history" widget="predict" config={weatherCliffPredict} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Lock in a bet before reading on: the single sentence of history is that the land sank or the
    sea rose, in that order — conglomerate to sandstone to shale to limestone is one
    flooding story told upward. Notice which principle lets five rock names carry a
    timeline: superposition, younger layers always on top.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "fossils can be found in any rock if you dig deep
  enough." Fossils are almost exclusively a sedimentary phenomenon. Igneous rock is born
  hot enough to vaporise them, and metamorphism's heat and pressure distort or erase
  them; only the gentle burial of sediment preserves remains — which is precisely why
  fossils double as environmental evidence, telling you the hosting rock must have
  formed at a surface where living things were.</p>

  <h2>Sedimentary structures and environments: the grammar gets precise</h2>
  <p>
    Sediments preserve <strong>structures</strong> — the shapes imposed while they were
    deposited — and each structure is a clue. <strong>Bedding</strong> (layers) is the
    default; each bed is one episode. <strong>Cross-bedding</strong> — layers inclined
    inside a bed — is the dune's and the river bar's handwriting: the inclination records
    current direction, so ancient sandstones can be read as fossilised wind fields.
    <strong>Ripple marks</strong> preserve wave or current action; <strong>mud
    cracks</strong> prove a surface dried in air (a lake margin, a floodplain) — a
    palaeoclimate clue in one crack; <strong>graded beds</strong> (coarse below, fine
    above) record underwater avalanches called turbidites. Fossils are the ultimate
    structure: only sedimentary rocks reliably host them, and fossils plus structures
    together identify the <strong>depositional environment</strong> — the discipline
    called facies analysis.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example: a rock unit shows cross-bedded sandstone with wind-rounded grains,
    no shells, but reptile footprints. The verdict — a fossilised desert dune field —
    is exactly how the Permian sandstones of the American Southwest were interpreted.
    Whole extinct geographies are reconstructed from this grammar; it is how we know
    oceans sat where the Alps now stand.
  </p>

  <h2>Mass wasting: gravity's own department</h2>
  <p>
    Between weathering and river transport sits a transport agent the syllabus files
    separately: <strong>mass wasting</strong> — the downslope movement of rock and soil
    under gravity alone. The classes are named by speed and water content, and each has
    a readable scar. <strong>Rockfalls</strong> (instant, from cliffs — the talus cones
    beneath them); <strong>landslides</strong> (a coherent mass sliding on a failure
    plane — the 1963 Vajont disaster moved 270 million m³ of mountainside into a
    reservoir in under a minute); <strong>slumps</strong> (rotational slippage with
    curved scarps — a hillside giving way like a stiff cake); <strong>debris
    flows</strong> (water-saturated, fast, channel-hugging — the volcano-lahar that
    buried Armero, Colombia, in 1985); and <strong>soil creep</strong> (centimetres a
    year, visible as tilted fence posts and curved tree trunks — slow enough to ignore
    and steady enough to dominate hillside budgets). The usual trigger is water: it adds
    weight, lubricates failure planes and dissolves the cement between grains — which is
    why hazard maps that route roads and housing are, in essence, hydrology maps.
    Deforestation and slope cutting move the odds; the 2006 Guinsaugon landslide in the
    Philippines followed ten days of rain on a logged slope.
  </p>
  <p>
    Soil deserves its own paragraph because it is weathering's end product and
    agriculture's substrate. A mature <strong>soil profile</strong> layers the results:
    an O horizon of litter; an A horizon (topsoil) of mineral mixed with humus — the
    biologically active layer; a B horizon where clay and iron leached from above
    accumulate; a C horizon of rotten parent rock; then bedrock. The profile builds at
    millimetres per year — centuries for a handful of centimetres — and can be lost in
    one bare winter, which is why contour ploughing, terracing and cover crops are
    erosion policy as much as agronomy. Soil type records the climate that made it:
    deep red laterites in the wet tropics (everything leached away but iron),
    chernozem's metre of black topsoil under grasslands (the world's breadbaskets), thin
    aridosols under deserts — a map of past weather drawn in the ground.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A hillside shows a heap of angular boulders beneath a cracked cliff, unmoved for centuries. Which process or processes are demonstrated?</li>
    <li>Why is sandstone usually made of quartz, when quartz is a minority of the crust?</li>
    <li>You find cross-bedded sandstone with wind-rounded grains and reptile footprints. What environment deposited it?</li>
    <li>Why do levees, flood walls and river straightening tend to worsen flooding downstream — and what does that have to do with deposition's size law?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Weathering without erosion — frost wedging fragmented
    the cliff in place, but nothing carried the debris away yet. Weathering and erosion
    are different stages, and this cliff shows only the first. (2) Quartz resists weak
    acids and oxygen, is hard, and has no cleavage — everything else in the parent rock
    weathers to clay or dissolves, so quartz is what survives to be deposited as sand.
    (3) A desert dune field — the inclination of cross-beds records wind direction, the
    rounded grains record grain-on-grain wind abrasion, and reptile footprints say dry
    land, not water. (4) Straightening raises banks and speeds water downstream, moving
    the flood problem rather than solving it; and because fast water carries coarse
    sediment, the accelerated flow both erodes upstream and dumps a coarser, higher
    load where it finally slows. (This question previews the water-cycle lesson's flood
    section — the two lessons meet there.)
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Weathering breaks rock in place; erosion transports the pieces; deposition sets them down sorted by size — three stages of one demolition-and-filing system.</li>
    <li>Mechanical weathering (frost wedging, thermal cycling, exfoliation, roots) multiplies surfaces; chemical weathering (dissolution, hydrolysis, oxidation) consumes them; each accelerates the other.</li>
    <li>Water erodes by hydraulic action and abrasion, ice by plucking, wind by sandblasting, waves by refraction-focused attack on headlands — each agent leaves a signature landform.</li>
    <li>The size law — fast flows carry big particles, slow flows only fine ones — builds fans, floodplains, deltas and beaches, and sorts sediment downstream gravel → sand → silt → clay.</li>
    <li>Lithification turns sediment into rock; clastic, chemical and organic sedimentary rocks classify by origin and grain size, and shale is the most abundant.</li>
    <li>Bedding, cross-bedding, ripples, mud cracks and fossils identify the depositional environment — sedimentary rocks are photographs of ancient places, stacked in order.</li>
    <li>Mass wasting moves material downhill under gravity alone, usually triggered by water; soil, weathering's end product, builds in centuries and washes away in a storm.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sediment filed by these processes is the raw material of the rock cycle's next
    act: deep burial turns it metamorphic, melting turns it igneous, and the lesson on
    the rock cycle closes the loop. The stratigraphic record you learned to read here is
    also geology's calendar — the lesson on geologic time shows how layers across whole
    continents are correlated into one timeline. And if you came here from the water
    cycle, notice the two lessons are one story: the rivers that return water to the sea
    are the same rivers filing the continents away.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, landscape evolution is a <strong>budget</strong>: uplift in,
    denudation out. Typical erosion rates on stable continental cores run about 10–100
    metres per million years; active mountain ranges shed 0.1–1 mm per year — enough to
    remove an Alps in roughly 50 million years if uplift stopped, which is why mountain
    ranges are not eras but standoffs. Rivers do most of the moving (about 19 billion
    tonnes of sediment to the oceans annually), glaciers punch far above their coverage,
    and the <strong>human acceleration</strong> is now the headline: agriculture and
    construction move more sediment than all natural processes combined (Wilkinson's
    2005 estimate — the "Anthropocene" argument in one measurement), while eroding
    farmland at 10–100 times the rate soil forms.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two deeper nuances. First, <strong>climate writes the style</strong>: the same
    granite weathers into rounded boulders in the hot wet tropics (chemical dominance),
    angular debris in polar ranges (frost dominance), and desert pavement under
    wind-only regimes — geomorphology is palaeoclimatology written on the ground, which
    is how the ancient glacial striations of India, Africa and Brazil helped prove
    continental drift. Second, the <strong>sedimentary archive is selective</strong>:
    erosion erases as much as deposition records — most of deep time is missing from any
    one place — so reading the archive requires correlating records across continents.
    The Grand Canyon is the best classroom for both lessons at once: a kilometre-deep
    absence whose walls preserve 1.5 billion years of shallow seas, deserts and deltas
    in the order they happened.
  </p>

